============================================================
SECTION 3: Mean vs Percentiles — Why the Mean Lies
============================================================

Scenario: a model serving system processes requests with these latencies (ms):
  [10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10, 1000]
  (9 fast requests, 1 slow due to a garbage collection pause)

  Mean   = (9 × 10 + 1000) / 10 = 109 ms  ← MISLEADING
  P50    = 10 ms                            ← typical user experience
  P90    = 10 ms                            ← 90% of users see this
  P99    = 1000 ms                          ← 1 in 100 users sees this

The mean obscures the tail. In a system serving 1,000 requests/second,
P99 means 10 users per second are getting 100× worse service than average.

Simulated distribution: 990 fast (50±5 ms) + 10 slow (800±50 ms):
  Mean :     57.4 ms  ← inflated by tail
  P50  :     50.0 ms  ← typical user
  P90  :     56.8 ms
  P95  :     59.1 ms
  P99  :     68.8 ms  ← worst 1%

Rule: always report at least P50 and P99. Never report only the mean.

============================================================
SECTION 4: Coefficient of Variation (CV)
============================================================

CV = std_dev / mean × 100%

CV tells you how noisy your benchmark is relative to its own magnitude.
It is unit-free, so you can compare stability across different workloads.

  CV < 2%   — excellent, your result is reliable
  CV 2-5%   — acceptable for most benchmarks
  CV 5-10%  — something is variable (thermal throttle? background load?)
  CV > 10%  — do not report this result, find and fix the noise source

  Scenario                                     Mean   StdDev     CV % Verdict
  ---------------------------------------- -------- -------- -------- ---------------
  Stable (thermal steady state)                50.1      0.1      0.2              OK
  Mild variability (mixed load)                50.5      3.0      6.0           check
  Noisy (thermal throttling)                   55.0     12.9     23.5           NOISY
  Bimodal (warmup contamination)               65.6     47.2     72.0         warmup!

============================================================
SECTION 5: Amdahl's Law — What Speedup Is Actually Possible?
============================================================

Amdahl's Law (1967): if you speed up a fraction P of your workload by S×,
the overall end-to-end speedup is:

  Speedup = 1 / ((1 - P) + P/S)

Where:
  P = fraction of time spent on the part you are optimizing
  S = speedup of that specific part
  (1 - P) = the part you are NOT changing (serial fraction)

Example: if the GPU kernel runs 10× faster but only occupies 80% of runtime,
  overall speedup = 1 / (0.2 + 0.8/10) = 1 / 0.28 = 3.57×  (not 10×!)

GPU kernel speedup assumed: 4×

  GPU fraction (P)       Serial fraction (1-P)    End-to-end speedup
  ---------------------- ------------------------ --------------------
      20%                    80%                      1.18×
      50%                    50%                      1.60×
      80%                    20%                      2.50×
      90%                    10%                      3.08×
      95%                     5%                      3.48×
      99%                     1%                      3.88×
     100%                     0%                      4.00×

Key lesson: optimizing the GPU kernel matters only if the GPU is the
bottleneck. If DataLoader occupies 50% of wall time, a 10× GPU speedup
delivers only 1.67× end-to-end improvement.

============================================================
SECTION 6: Benchmark Type Comparison
============================================================
  Type                         Scope                        Strength                       Weakness
  ---------------------------- ---------------------------- ------------------------------ -----------------------------------
  Microbenchmark               Single kernel or op          Isolates one variable          Not representative of full workload
  End-to-end synthetic         Full model, synthetic data   Repeatable, no data prep       May not match real input distribution
  Prod trace replay            Real request log replayed    Most realistic                 Hard to reproduce, privacy concerns
  Parametrised replay          Synthetic data, real shape distribution Reproducible + realistic       Requires shape analysis upfront

============================================================
SECTION 7: A Rigorous Timing Harness
============================================================

Every benchmark result you report must include:
  1. Measurement method (CUDA events / CPU wall clock / perf counters)
  2. Warmup iterations (how many runs discarded before recording)
  3. Sample count (how many stable measurements taken)
  4. P50 and P99 (not just mean)
  5. CV% (to confirm the benchmark was stable)

Below is the harness pattern used throughout this book:

Running GPU timing harness demo...
  Operation   : torch.mm(1024, 1024)
  Warmup runs : 10
  Sample runs : 50
  Measurement : CUDA events (device-side, not CPU wall clock)
  Mean        : 0.242 ms
  P50         : 0.238 ms
  P99         : 0.437 ms
  CV          : 13.37%  ← check variability

